  "slug": "protein-golgi-1670",
  "summary": "Recent preprints quantify regulatory change across states: super-enhancer rewiring, liver zonation loss, senescence drivers and tumor pathway deregulation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is quantifying how regulatory organization shifts between conditions rather than cataloguing static regulators. At the chromatin level, enhancer–promoter Hi-C network analysis across three differentiation systems finds that super-enhancers rewire more than other elements and connect to identity genes. Two liver studies take a spatial approach using spatial transcriptomics. One applies causal-discovery ML algorithms to Visium data to separate local from neighbor-derived causes and effects of cellular senescence. The other uses spatial correlation functions, extracting per-gene susceptibility and correlation length to characterize zonation loss in fibrotic versus healthy tissue. A pathway-level extension of the N/T-gene framework scores Reactome deregulation, builds a taxonomy via minimum spanning trees and compares normal with tumor tissue. Across these methods, the shift is toward explicitly condition-contrastive, network- or field-based descriptors of regulation. Spatial causality and physics-style correlation metrics are the more methodologically novel contributions.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
